    {
      "step_id": "tile_data_shape_and_types.s05",
      "type": "explainer",
      "concept_ids": [
        "data.categorical_numeric_types"
      ],
      "eyebrow": "Variable Types",
      "title": "Categorical and numerical variables",
      "lede": "Not all columns work the same way — the type of a column decides what you're allowed to do with it.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_data_shape_and_types.s05.b01",
          "role": "intro",
          "text": "Look at two columns in the Kwik Courier table: delivery_min (say, 32 minutes) and city (say, 'Lagos'). Both are columns in the same table, but they behave completely differently. Knowing which kind of column you're looking at changes what you're allowed to do with it.",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#tb1"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#f1",
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#f2"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_data_shape_and_types.s05.b02",
          "role": "definition",
          "text": "A numerical variable takes numerical values for which it is sensible to add or subtract. delivery_min is numerical: 32 minutes plus 41 minutes means something real, and the difference between two delivery times is a meaningful quantity. A categorical variable's values are categories — also called levels. city is categorical: its values ('Lagos', 'Abuja', 'Kano') are labels for a group; there is nothing to add or subtract between them.",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#tb2"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#f1",
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#f2"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_data_shape_and_types.s05.b03",
          "role": "misconception",
          "text": "A column stored as numbers is not automatically numerical. In the Kwik Courier data, zone_code holds 101, 102, 103 — but adding two zone codes together produces nothing useful, just as adding two telephone area codes would. zone_code is a label, not a quantity, so it is categorical despite being written as digits. If arithmetic on the values would be meaningless, the column is categorical.",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#tb3"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "data.categorical_numeric_types#m1"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
